---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Trung Duc Nguyen — software engineer based in Berlin">
  <div class="narrative">
    <div class="hi reveal">Hey there</div>
    <h1 class="lead reveal reveal-delay-1">
      I'm <em>Trung</em>, a software engineer based in Berlin. By day I'm building self-service banking products at Deutsche Bank. By night I'm building <em>agentic developer tools</em> with Claude Code.
    </h1>

    <div class="body reveal reveal-delay-2">
      <p>
        I studied <span class="hl">Information Systems Management</span> at TU Berlin and have been working in software since 2018. Most of my day-job energy goes into <span class="hl">commercial banking on GCP</span> &mdash; backend services in Java and Spring Boot, cross-team integration, and CI automation.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've spent the last year going deep on what it means to actually work <em>with</em> AI agents &mdash; not just chat with them. The job isn't getting an LLM to write more code; it's building the surrounding scaffolding so the LLM does the right thing, every time. I've found that's mostly a <span class="hl">design</span> problem.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>Deutsche Bank</h3>
        <div class="when">2025 &mdash; Now</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            I'm the primary backend developer on <em>Phoenix (Front-to-Back)</em>, a platform automating self-service products for commercial banking and expanding into new European markets. First deliverable: <span class="hl">B2B SEPA mandate management</span> serving 45,000+ corporate clients in Germany.
          </p>
          <p>
            I drive cross-team integration across 3 internal platform teams, co-built CI automation that cut frontend deployment from 30&ndash;60 min to 2&ndash;5 min, and co-designed a dynamic document generation system that eliminated per-brand template storage entirely.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>Side Projects</h3>
        <div class="when">2025 &mdash; Now</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            On the side I build tools that exercise the agent-engineering thesis. <a href="/projects">agent-eng</a> is a published npm package that scaffolds agentic workflows into any project &mdash; five specialized subagents, ADR templates, ticket structure, all from a single <code>npx</code> command. <a href="/projects">AI_TTRPG</a> is a real-time voice-powered D&amp;D Game Master where players speak via WebRTC and combat resolves through a deterministic rules engine. <a href="/projects">Project Hub</a> is a React workflow visualizer for editing agentic pipelines and system architectures.
          </p>
          <p>
            The throughline is the same: <span class="hl">give the agent fewer, sharper levers</span>. The model is the chef. Your job is to set the kitchen.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>cliqe</h3>
        <div class="when">2023</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            Founders Associate at an early-stage startup in the creator/brand space. Built onboarding automation with TypeScript and Firebase that cut onboarding from 3 minutes to 30 seconds, and a Python outreach system that scaled daily reach from 600 to 1,000.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>Capgemini</h3>
        <div class="when">2018 &mdash; 2019, 2022 &mdash; 2023</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            Two stints. First as a <em>QA Engineer</em> on a complex Java automotive system &mdash; JUnit testing, MySQL query optimization, collaborating across teams in Germany and India. Later as an <em>ERP Business Analyst</em>, writing user stories for an Oracle ERP implementation and establishing a Confluence documentation process for 20+ project documents.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>Academic Tutor</h3>
        <div class="when">2019 &mdash; 2021</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            Taught technical foundations and advanced Java programming at TU Berlin. Graded work on concurrency, deadlock prevention, and thread synchronization &mdash; topics that still shape how I think about backend systems.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>TU Berlin</h3>
        <div class="when">2017 &mdash; 2025</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            B.Sc. Business Informatics, then M.Sc. Information Systems Management. The degrees gave me half engineering, half product context &mdash; the half I keep reaching for when reviewing a PR or scoping a feature is, surprisingly often, the second one. Exchange year at Hanyang University in South Korea along the way.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>Off-screen</h3>
        <div class="when">Always</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            Outside of work I'm running tabletop campaigns, fighting with espresso ratios, or trying (and failing) to leave the apartment before noon on a Saturday. I think the most interesting problems sit at the boundaries between disciplines &mdash; which is also why I keep bringing TTRPG mechanics into software design and vice versa.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter reveal">
      <div>
        <h3>Get in touch</h3>
        <div class="when">Open</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            I'm not actively looking, but I love comparing notes on agent engineering, Claude Code, or how to ship LLM features that don't fall over in production. The fastest way is <a href="mailto:nguyendtrung15@gmail.com">email</a>. Code is on <a href="https://github.com/swarpi">GitHub</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .narrative { margin-top: 8vh; }

  .hi {
    font-family: 'Geist Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }

  .lead {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3vw, 38px);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: 1.3;
    text-wrap: pretty;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .lead em { font-style: italic; }

  .narrative > .body {
    margin-top: 40px;
    max-width: 620px;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .narrative > .body p { margin-bottom: 18px; }

  .body .hl {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent-soft);
    padding-bottom: 1px;
  }

  .body a {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-bottom: 1px;
    transition: color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s;
  }

  .body a:hover { color: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); }

  .chapter {
    margin-top: 56px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .chapter h3 {
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .chapter .when {
    font-family: 'Geist Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  .chapter .body { margin-top: 0; font-size: 16px; }
  .chapter .body p { margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .chapter .body p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .chapter { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; }
  }
</style>
